  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Saturday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: A parent low off the Pacific NW coast is\ndragging a very subtle shortwave across the Great Basin early\nSunday morning. There is very little moisture and/or forcing with\nthis system so anything more than flurries along the northern\nborder NV is not anticipated. Short-term high-res guidance has no\nmore than a dusting forecasted for mainly locations in northern\nHumboldt County. \n\nThe axis of the low to the north drags across the region Monday.\nWhat moisture there is associated with this system seems to be\ntrapped aloft with a significant dry layer to overcome according\nto sounding analysis in the area. Snowfall progs are rather low \nwith only a few hundreds of fresh accumulation expected along the\nnorthern NV border through Monday. A transitional ridge builds\nover the Great Basin Tuesday helping a warming trend that will\npush high temperatures over the area closer to seasonal normals. \n\nModel disagreement exists in the next wave forecasted to quickly\nmove into the region Wednesday evening. Long range models are\ndivergent on both moisture and forcing with this system. Current\nguidance keeps the area fairly dry with any snowfall confined\nagain to northern NV (northern Elko County). Ridging builds back\ninto the SW CONUS on Thanksgiving and through the end of the week\npushing high temperatures a few degrees above normal by Friday and\nSaturday.\n\nAll in all, a fairly active pattern across the western half of the\nCONUS continues but impacts (especially considering recent weather\nsystems through the area) are insignificant at best. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Moderate confidence exists in\nthe passage of a cold front on Monday but confidence has\ndiminished in precipitation chances as model runs continue to\nerode moisture availability with incoming systems.",
